{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":4190,"output":474,"cacheRead":30720,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":394,"totalTokens":35384,"cost":{"input":0.0006284999999999999,"output":0.000237,"cacheRead":0.0009216,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0017870999999999998}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Now let's check the current propose(): it skips ERR_* codes entirely. The brief requires: audit rows with code ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED / ERR_DOC_NOT_FINDABLE should route like any other ERR_* agent-fault row — meaning we need a bridge so doc-audit rows route. But there's also the report.rs agent-fault exclusion... How do doc-audit report rows enter the routing? The doc_audit report_row produces report rows with scenario \"doc-audit\" and code ERR_*. Currently, propose() would exclude ERR_* codes. We need new behavior: those two specific codes ARE routable as agent-fault... wait no. The brief says \"routes like any other ERR_* agent-fault row — fingerprint token evallerina:<scenario>:<fault-class>\". Hmm — \"like any other ERR_* agent-fault row\"? But agent-fault rows are currently NOT routed. Wait, read the brief again:\n\n\"a row with code ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED or ERR_DOC_NOT_FINDABLE routes like any other ERR_* agent-fault row — fingerprint token evallerina:<scenario>:<fault-class>, cross-model threshold min(3, |registry|) (existing propose() semantics reused verbatim — no new routing semantics per D3).\"\n\nHmm, that's ambiguous. I think it means: the audit rows are accepted as fault rows — they route through propose() machinery, with fingerprint token `evallerina:<scenario>:<fault-class>` where fault-class is the ERR code presumably. \"routes like any other ERR_* agent-fault row\" — maybe report.rs already has routing for agent faults? Let me check report.rs to see how routing interacts. Also check routing parity upstream flag handling — routing.toml now has upstream=true entries; parse_routing_toml ignores them, need to handle `skippe