{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now let me look at report.rs's routing step to understand how propose() gets rows, and how routing.toml upstream flag may need parsing. Also check parse_routing_toml — routing.toml now includes `upstream = true`, and parse only reads repo. We need to parse the upstream flag.\n\nDesign decisions:\n- propose() currently excludes all ERR_* codes. Doc-audit rows are report rows (report_version 1, scenario \"doc-audit\", tool = tool name, checks with code ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED/ERR_DOC_NOT_FINDABLE). We need propose() to route these two codes as routable faults with fault_class = the code itself (token `evallerina:<scenario>:<fault-class>` → `evallerina:doc-audit:ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED` per spec: \"token evallerina:S:ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED\"). So fault_class = the ERR code.\n\nWait — spec scenario says \"ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED for scenario S against tool T\" — so scenario may be arbitrary? The doc_audit report_row uses scenario \"doc-audit\" fixed. For routing tests, fault_class = code.\n\nSo changes to propose(): in the match on check code:\n- Some(code) if code == ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED || code == ERR_DOC_NOT_FINDABLE → route with fault_class = code.to_owned()\n- Some(code) other ERR_* → skip (agent faults)\n- None → tool_fault as before\n\nThreshold: same min(3, registry). For doc-audit rows, distinct models... but doc-audit rows from doc_audit.rs report_row have no \"model\" field! Look at report_row: it doesn't include a model. Hmm. propose() requires rows... model is optional (row.get(\"model\")). If no model, then entry.models stays empty and threshold never crosses. Hmm, that's a problem: audit rows lack model attribution.\n\nWait — spec says \"observed in trial rows from at least the threshold number of distinct models\". For doc-audit, the audit isn't per-model. Hmm. How does the routing threshold apply? For the smoke/e2e, maybe doc-audit rows carry a model? Actually the audit is tier-0, run once — no models. Threshold min(3, |registry|)... if no model attribution, fault never crosses.\n\nHmm, maybe the design intends: doc-audit rows should be attributed... Let me check report.rs — how do tier-1 rows without model attribution get handled in routing? The routing.rs doc comment says rows include \"tier-1 rows without model attribution\". Let me check report.rs aggregate & the routing step to see how rows without model are treated.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Now let me look at report.rs's routing step to understand how propose() gets rows, and how routing.toml upstream flag may need parsing. Also check parse_routing_toml — routing.toml now includes `upstream = true`, and parse only reads repo. We need to parse the upstream flag.\\n\\nDesign decisions:\\n- propose() currently excludes all ERR_* codes. Doc-audit rows are report rows (report_version 1, scenario \\\"doc-audit\\\", tool = tool name, checks with code ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED/ERR_DOC_NOT_FINDABLE). We need propose() to route these two codes as routable faults with fault_class = the code itself (token `evallerina:<scenario>:<fault-class>` → `evallerina:doc-audit:ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED` per spec: \\\"token evallerina:S:ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED\\\"). So fault_class = the ERR code.\\n\\nWait — spec scenario says \\\"ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED for scenario S against tool T\\\" — so scenario may be arbitrary? The doc_audit report_row uses scenario \\\"doc-audit\\\" fixed. For routing tests, fault_class = code.\\n\\nSo changes to propose(): in the match on check code:\\n- Some(code) if code == ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED || code == ERR_DOC_NOT_FINDABLE → route with fault_class = code.to_owned()\\n- Some(code) other ERR_* → skip (agent faults)\\n- None → tool_fault as before\\n\\nThreshold: same min(3, registry). For doc-audit rows, distinct models... but doc-audit rows from doc_audit.rs report_row have no \\\"model\\\" field! Look at report_row: it doesn't include a model. Hmm. propose() requires rows... model is optional (row.get(\\\"model\\\")). If no model, then entry.models stays empty and threshold never crosses. Hmm, that's a problem: audit rows lack model attribution.\\n\\nWait — spec says \\\"observed in trial rows from at least the threshold number of distinct models\\\". For doc-audit, the audit isn't per-model. Hmm. How does the routing threshold apply? For the smoke/e2e, maybe doc-audit rows carry a model? Actually the audit is tier-0, run once — no models. Threshold min(3, |registry|)... if no model attribution, fault never crosses.\\n\\nHmm, maybe the design intends: doc-audit rows should be attributed... Let me check report.rs — how do tier-1 rows without model attribution get handled in routing? The routing.rs doc comment says rows include \\\"tier-1 rows without model attribution\\\". Let me check report.rs aggregate & the routing step to see how rows without model are treated.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_90e08128832a4ddd943b3343","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /var/home/sasha/para/areas/dev/gh/charly/evallerina && grep -n \"model\\|run_routing_step\\|propose\" src/report.rs | head -50","timeout":30}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","usage":{"input":807,"output":608,"cacheRead":36608,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":550,"totalTokens":38023,"cost":{"input":0.00012104999999999999,"output":0.00030399999999999996,"cacheRead":0.00109824,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0015232899999999998}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1792528745215,"responseId":"gen-1792528745-wrv4CAMpwYgrFRPO5Z6U","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_90e08128832a4ddd943b3343","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4://! events and tier-1 rows without model attribution) into a\n5://! per-output-channel × check-code × model-id fault matrix, apply the\n7://! faults → AIX-