{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"\"This change does NOT touch that machinery — it feeds it more scenario families\". So routing accepts doc-audit code rows as routable faults. The threshold semantics: min(3, |registry|) distinct models.\n\nFor the doc-audit tier-0 report rows (no model field), they never cross the threshold... unless the smoke attributes models. Hmm. But the brief explicitly wants a dry-run smoke with proposals written as evidence. Maybe proposals can be empty and that's fine — the evidence file exists and is committed. But \"proposals reviewed\" — if empty, nothing to review; that would make ticket 3.2 trivially satisfied. Hmm, but maybe that's actually correct today: doc-audit rows are findings, not cross-model faults — routing them without models shouldn't file tickets. But then task 3.1's failing test uses model-attributed rows.\n\nWait — maybe a cleaner approach that keeps propose() verbatim: the doc-audit CLI wiring synthesizes the routing intake by attributing each audit row to... no.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe propose() should treat audit rows (code ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED/ERR_DOC_NOT_FINDABLE) as routable with model attribution absent meaning \"all configured models\"? That IS new semantics. The brief's follow-up threshold says: if machinery resists accepting audit rows without semantic changes >1h, STOP and record impedance. So there's a known possibility that this is awkward.\n\nPragmatic reading of the red/green steps:\n1. RED: \"audit row with ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED over threshold\" — construct doc-audit report rows carrying model attribution (simulating tier-2 doc-findability trial rows, which is what task 4 will produce), 3 distinct models → proposal with token `evallerina:<scenario>:ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED`, fault_class = \"ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED\".\n2. Same for ERR_DOC_NOT_FINDABLE.\n3. RED: upstream=true tool's audit row → skipped/unmapped_upstream. This requires parse_routing_toml to expose upstream flag, and propose() to use it: when repo mapped but upstream=true → decision skipped, reason \"unmapped_upstream\".\n4. RED: dedup — same token twice → one proposal. Note: propose() already collapses same (scenario, fault_class, tool) key across rows — dedup by BTreeMap key. So feeding two rows with same key yields one proposal. Test proves it.\n5. CLI wiring: doc-audit dry-run — route audit rows; since audit rows have no model, propose gives zero proposals. Write proposed-tickets.jsonl (possibly empty) to research dir.\n\nHmm wait, but for step 5 the smoke: \"cargo run -- doc-audit → dry-run proposals JSONL written to .wai/projects/evallerina/research/ as evidence\". If it's empty, evidence is weak but the exit is 0 and file written. Acceptable? The gate says \"dry-run proposals JSONL written\". I'll make the smoke path always write the JSONL (even empty) and also emit the routed summary.\n\nAlternatively, better: make the doc-audit routing intake model-attribute rows to the configured registry count as one occurrence each with... no. Let me think about whether propose() could treat a model-less routable row as attributable to the entire registry. Consider: audit runs are tier-0, single-shot; the doc finding applies to the whole battery. If we attribute to all configured models, threshold auto-crosses (registry ≥ 3 → threshold 3; distinct models = registry size ≥ 3 → crosses). Is that \"new routing semantics\"? It's a new intake rule but not new propose() semantics — it's a row-shape bridge: the bridge function in doc_audit.rs expands each audit row into one row per configured model? Hmm, that's fabricating provenance that didn't happen — lying in provenance. Anti-goal-ish: provenance must be truthful.\n\nBut the spec scenario says \"observed in trial rows from at least the threshold number of distinct models\" — that's about future tier-2 doc-findability scenarios. The tier-0 audit rows crossing into filing would require... hmm, actually the spec requirement \"Fault routing reuses existing machinery\" — scenario \"Undocumented feature files ticket in tool repo\": WHEN ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED ... observed in trial rows from ≥ threshold distinct models AND operator runs report --route --file. So filing doc faults requires model-attributed trial rows. That confirms: tier-0 audit rows alone don't file; only trial rows (from doc-findability scenarios, task 4) do.\n\nSo for the doc-audit CLI dry-run: feed the doc-audit report rows through propose() — they have no models → no proposals cross threshold → empty JSONL. That is the honest dry-run today. The smoke evidence = the empty (or non-empty) JSONL + maybe a summary.\n\nHmm wait, but hold on — would propose() even accept doc-audit report rows? They have scenario \"doc-audit\", tool <tool>, no model, report_version 1, checks with code. scenario_tool_of(\"doc-audit\") — routing keys the repo on scenario's recorded tool; for doc-audit rows the tool is IN the row. The existing propose() ignores row tool for repo keying and requires scenario_tool_of(scenario) to resolve. For scenario \"doc-audit\" there is no recorded tool. Hmm! That's an impedance: propose() would loud-error \"scenario 'doc-audit' has no recorded tool\".\n\nSo the bridge: for doc-audit rows, the \"scenario\" for routing... The spec says token `evallerina:S:ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED` for scenario S against tool T. For tier-0 audit rows, scenario is \"doc-audit\" and the tool is the row's tool. So the bridge function should map doc-audit rows into routing rows where the repo keys on the row's tool (the audited tool), not a scenario-recorded tool.\n\nHow to do this with minimal diff and no new semantics? Option: extend propose() so that for routable doc codes, the tool key comes from the row's tool field (which IS the audited tool in doc-audit rows) instead of scenario_tool_of. But the hhs-bug comment says never trust row tool... for live rows. For doc-audit rows, row tool is the truth. Hmm, \"no new routing semantics\" is about threshold/dedup/token semantics, not about intake.\n\nSimplest approach honoring everything:\n- In propose(), the match on check code: `Some(code) if is_doc_code(code)` → routable fault with fault_class = code. For the tool key: use scenario_tool_of(scenario) as before? For test 3.1 the scenario can be a doc-audit scenario name... but unknown scenarios are loud errors. The test would need scenario_tool_of to resolve the scenario. E.g. test defines recorded tool mapping \"wai-doc-drift-stale-agents-md\" → \"wai\"? But audit rows have scenario \"doc-audit\" fixed...\n\nHmm wait. Actually let me reconsider what the routing intake for doc-audit rows should be. The bridge function (brief: \"src/doc_audit.rs — only if a row-shape bridge function is needed there\") could rewrite each doc-audit report row into a routing row keyed per tool: scenario stays \"doc-audit\", tool = audited tool. And propose() for doc codes keys on the row's tool (bridged).\n\nDesign decision: in propose(), for doc-code faults, use the row's `tool` field as the repo key (the audited tool is authoritative in doc-audit rows) — and still require scenario_tool_of to resolve? No: scenario \"doc-audit\" has no recorded tool. Skip scenario_tool_of for doc rows. That's a branch, but the routing semantics (threshold, token, dedup, dry-run) remain verbatim.\n\nActually, cleaner: keep ONE code path. In propose():\n\n```rust\nmatch check.get(\"code\").and_then(...) {\n    Some(code) if code == ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED || code == ERR_DOC_NOT_FINDABLE => {\n        // Doc-coverage faults (evallerina-fok/e4x): the audited tool is\n        // the row's tool field (doc-audit rows carry it authoritatively);\n        // the fault class is the check code itself.\n        route fault with tool = row_tool, fault_class = code\n    }\n    Some(_) => continue, // other agent faults never routed\n    None => tool_fault with recorded_tool\n}\n```\n\nBut then the scenario_tool_of resolution must not fire for doc rows... it's computed before the checks loop. Currently `recorded_tool` computed per row and error if unknown. For doc-audit rows, scenario \"doc-audit\" unknown → error. So we need to move resolution or special-case. Restructure: resolve recorded_tool lazily only for tool-fault (code-less) checks. I.e., move the scenario_tool_of call into the None branch. But then a row with zero failing checks never errors on unknown scenario... Currently unknown scenario errors even if all checks pass. Hmm — behavior change: previously an unknown scenario with all-passing checks errors. Tests may pin that (\"unknown_scenario_is_a_loud_error\" uses a failing check row). Changing to lazy keeps that test green (it has a failing code-less check). But is lazy resolution acceptable? It's tidy: resolution only needed when a tool fault exists. I'll do lazy resolution inside the None arm.\n\nWait, but also rows with model-less doc-audit rows: provenance requires model; audit rows lack model → entry.models empty → filtered by threshold. Fine for real smoke (empty proposals). Test 3.1 uses model-attributed rows.\n\nHmm, but hold on: is the smoke then trivially empty? Let me check what `cargo run -- doc-audit` currently outputs — do any real ERR rows exist? Let me run it.\n\nAlso dedup test (step 4): \"same fingerprint token twice → one proposal\" — propose() BTreeMap already collapses. Test: two rows same scenario+code+tool → one proposal. That's the proof.\n\nUpstream flag: parse_routing_toml currently ignores unknown fields — good (upstream=true parses fine today). Need to expose upstream: change return type to include upstream flag, e.g. BTreeMap<String, RepoEntry{repo, upstream}>? That changes propose() signature and report.rs call site and routing_parity usage. Let me check usages of parse_routing_toml.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"\\\"This change does NOT touch that machinery — it feeds it more scenario famili