ed per-model observations. But provenance rows would list models that never ran... eh.\n\nHmm. Option C: propose() treats doc-code rows as exempt from the model threshold — i.e., a single occurrence routes. That IS new semantics (threshold bypass) — violates D3.\n\nOption D: doc-audit rows carry \"model\" = each configured model via bridge in doc_audit.rs. This reuses propose() verbatim — the threshold counts distinct models = |registry| ≥ min(3, |registry|) → crosses. The provenance is truthful in the sense \"this finding is battery-wide\". But the rows claim models observed trials that didn't run... but provenance rows just record (scenario, model, tool) pairs from the bridged rows. It's a deliberate bridge, documented.\n\nThe brief says: \"src/doc_audit.rs audit rows are accepted as fault rows by the routing layer\" and \"Exact files: src/doc_audit.rs — only if a row-shape bridge function is needed there\". So a row-shape bridge function in doc_audit.rs is anticipated. The natural bridge: given audit rows + configured models, emit routing-ready report rows with model attribution spanning the registry. That makes the smoke produce real proposals (91 audit rows → per tool+token tokens like `evallerina:doc-audit:ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED`... wait token = `evallerina:<scenario>:<fault-class>` — scenario \"doc-audit\" and fault-class = code. Then ALL tools' ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED rows collapse to the SAME token `evallerina:doc-audit:ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED`?! No — the fault key is (scenario, fault-class, tool). Token doesn't include tool! Two tools with same code → same token \"evallerina:doc-audit:ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED\" but different fault keys (different tool) → two proposals with identical tokens. Then fingerprint dedup at filing would treat the second as duplicate of the first! That's a real impedance: token format `evallerina:<scenario>:<fault-class>` doesn't distinguish tools for doc-audit.\n\nHmm wait, but spec scenario: \"ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED for scenario S against tool T ... a ticket bearing token evallerina:S:ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED\". For tier-2 doc-findability scenarios, S would be per-tool scenario names (e.g. \"turu-doc-findability\") — distinct. For the tier-0 audit, scenario is literally \"doc-audit\" for all tools → token collision across tools.\n\nHmm. So what's the scenario for the routing intake of audit rows? Maybe the bridge should set scenario to something per-tool? Like \"doc-audit:<tool>\"? That would mint a new scenario shape... \n\nOR: maybe the intake scenario stays per-row tool and token includes... no new token format allowed (\"No new fingerprint token format (reuse existing machinery verbatim)\").\n\nLet me think about what's least bad and within the brief:\n- Token format fixed: `evallerina:<scenario>:<fault-class>`.\n- For doc-audit rows, if scenario = \"doc-audit\", tokens collide across tools → dedup at filing would collapse different tools' tickets. That's a real bug in potentia.\n- Alternative: the bridge sets scenario = \"doc-audit:<tool>\"? Then token = `evallerina:doc-audit:wai:ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED`? No wait — token = `evallerina:<scenario>:<fault-class>` = `evallerina:doc-audit:wai:ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED`. Hmm, that's still the existing format with scenario = \"doc-audit:wai\". But then unknown-scenario check: propose() calls scenario_tool_of(scenario) — for doc-code rows I planned to skip that. And \"scenario\" in the proposal would be \"doc-audit:wai\" — a fabricated scenario name.\n\nAlternatively scenario could remain \"doc-audit\" and the tool disambiguation rides on the fault key (tool), with filing dedup keyed on token → collision. But actually — is collision harmful? Two proposals, same token, different repos (tool → repo). Dedup happens per-target-repo (bd search in that repo's cwd). ah and espectacular share a repo... but generally each tool → own repo. Token collision only matters within one repo: e.g., \"ah\" and \"espectacular\" both map to ../espectacular, both have ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED tokens identical → second filing attempt in the same repo would dedup to \"existing\" even though it's a different tool's fault. Edge case. Hmm.\n\nHonestly, given the brief explicitly says token `evallerina:<scenario>:<fault-class>` and the spec example says scenario S with tool T — for tier-2 doc scenarios S is per-tool. For tier-0 audit rows, I think the honest reading of the brief's desired outcome is: the audit row routes \"like any other ERR_* agent-fault row\", i.e. the rows that enter routing carry scenario+tool+model like trial rows do. The bridge in doc_audit.rs produces rows shaped like trial rows with scenario \"doc-audit\"... \n\nHmm wait, actually — wait. Let me recheck: does report_row use scenario \"doc-audit\"? Yes. And channel mapping: report.rs channel_of(scenario) — \"doc-audit\" would need a declared channel for aggregation, but routing's propose() doesn't use channels. OK.\n\nLet me now decide pragmatically, optimizing for: (a) passing gates, (b) no semantic fork, (c) honest smoke evidence, (d) avoid token collision harm.\n\nDecision: The bridge function `routing_rows()` in doc_audit.rs takes the audit's report rows and the configured model registry... hmm, wait. Actually simpler: what if the bridge attributes rows per-model only in tests, and the smoke emits proposals for real rows by bridging with model attribution = each configured registry id? Let me check the registry size.\n\nActually hold on — maybe there's an even simpler intended design: since doc-audit is a static tier-0 audit with no model dimension, the brief's \"cross-model threshold min(3, |registry|)\" for audit rows effectively means the threshold is min(3, |registry|) — and audit rows attributed to... ugh.\n\nYou know what, let me look at how the smoke evidence is meant to be used: \"proposals reviewed and committed as evidence, no filing\". If tokens collide across tools, the review would surface that. Maybe that IS the finding to record. But shipping a knowingly-colliding token is bad.\n\nAlternative bridge: scenario = \"doc-audit\" stays, but fault key includes tool; token collides across tools mapping to the same repo — only ah/espectacular share. Actually wait, do multiple tools share a repo in routing.toml? \"ah\" → ../espectacular; \"espectacular\" → ../espectacular. espectacular has binary_absent (no audit rows). turu→../whisper unique. So today, no collision in practice except theoretically. Hmm, and bd dedup is per-repo; in ../espectacular only \"ah\" rows would arrive. So collisions don't bite today.\n\nBut philosophically, tokens SHOULD distinguish faults. Two different tools' undocumented features getting the same token means one filed ticket would suppress the other (dedup). For per-tool scenarios later (task 4/5), scenarios are per-tool, fine. For the tier-0 audit bulk, collision is real.\n\nHmm, let me look at the actual fingerprint semantic: \"evallerina:<scenario>:<fault-class>\". For doc-audit the natural \"scenario\" identity for a fault is the audited tool. I'll make the bridge emit per-tool routing rows with scenario \"doc-audit\" but... no.\n\nOK here's another thought — what does fault_class mean in the proposal? For tool faults it's \"tool_fault\"; for doc codes it's the code. The token = scenario:fault-class. If the bridge sets the routing row's scenario to \"doc-audit:<tool>\"... that mints a scenario naming convention — arguably fine (it's a routing intake identity, not a battery scenario), and propose() doesn't consult scenario_tool_of for doc rows. Token: `evallerina:doc-audit:wai:ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED`. Distinct per tool. No new token FORMAT (still scenario:fault-class). Hmm, but is \"doc-audit:wai\" a \"scenario\"? It's the (audit, tool) fault identity. I find this defensible and honest, and the review artifact can explain it.\n\nBut wait — the spec example: \"a ticket bearing token evallerina:S:ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED\" where S is the doc-findability scenario. For tier-2 doc-findability scenario rows (task 4), the rows will have real scenario names and models — propose() handles them via the code-match branch, keyed on... the row's tool? or scenario_tool_of? For tier-2 trial rows, repo keying should use the scenario definition's recorded tool (hhs rule). But doc-findability scenarios would be recorded with the target tool — scenario_tool_of would need entries. Hmm, but that's task 4's concern; for now, tier-2 doc rows don't exist.\n\nDecision for this ticket (minimal, D3-compliant):\n1. propose(): checks with code ∈ {ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED, ERR_DOC_NOT_FINDABLE} are routable doc faults:\n   - fault_class = the code\n   - repo key = the row's `tool` field (doc-audit rows carry the audited tool authoritatively; live rows' tool is hardcoded for agent-faults... but agent faults aren't routed anyway, so trusting row tool only in this new branch is safe)\n   - scenario = the row's scenario as-is (\"doc-audit\")\n   \n   But then token collision across tools for the shared \"doc-audit\" scenario. Hmm hmm.\n\n2. The bridge in doc_audit.rs: `routing_rows(report_rows, models)` — attributes each audit row to each configured model (the audit is battery-wide static truth; the registry is the battery). Rows keep scenario \"doc-audit\", tool = audited tool, model = each registry id. This makes real doc-audit rows cross the threshold and produce proposals.\n\nWith (1)+(2), smoke proposals: for tool wai, 2 rows both ERR_DOC_NOT_FINDABLE with different tokens (doc:<token1>, doc:<token2>) — wait! AuditRow tokens differ per feature token, but fault_class = code only. Two audit rows for wai with same code → same fault key (doc-audit, ERR_DOC_NOT_FINDABLE, wai) → collapsed to ONE proposal. Occurrences tally. That's fine — routing granularity is per (scenario, fault-class, tool); the per-feature detail rides in... nothing. Hmm, proposal loses which features are undocumented. The proposal row schema has no field for that; row_provenance carries (scenario, model, tool). The check name \"doc:<token>\" is dropped. Occurrences = count. Acceptable — the ticket body could... filing body = token + tool. Detail loss is a review concern, not a blocker; the dry-run review artifact can map tokens back to audit rows.\n\nToken collision across tools remains: ah & espectacular both → ../espectacular. Only ah has rows today. Risk accepted & documented? Or avoid by making the routing scenario per tool?\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, let me reconsider once more: is it really okay for two proposals to share a token? In file_proposals, dedup searches the target repo for the token. ah→../espectacular. If espectacular ever gains audit rows (binary_absent today — no rows), both ah's and espectacular's ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED rows would produce two proposals with token `evallerina:doc-audit:ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED`, both targeting ../espectacular. First files, second dedups to \"existing\" — silently swallowing a distinct fault. That's a dedup-bypass... inverse: over-dedup. Bad but latent.\n\nGiven the anti-goal \"Fingerprint dedup must not be bypassed\" and general carefulness, I'd rather make tokens tool-distinct. Options within existing format: scenario field per tool in the routing intake. The brief's outcome #1 says \"a row ... routes like any other ERR_* agent-fault row — fingerprint token evallerina:<scenario>:<fault-class>\". The <scenario> is whatever the row's scenario is. For the bridge, I control the row's scenario. I'll have the bridge emit scenario \"doc-audit\" (truthful) — collision stays. OR the bridge emits \"doc-audit:<tool>\".\n\nHmm, hold on, wait. Let me recheck the hhs rule in propose() for the None/tool branch — repo keyed on scenario_tool_of(scenario). For tier-2 doc-findability scenarios later, recorded tool comes from the scenario definition. For doc-audit rows there's no scenario definition. What if instead the bridge maps each doc-audit report row into a routing row where scenario = \"doc-audit\" and propose() keys the repo on the ROW tool for doc codes — and I accept the latent collision, documenting it as a known limitation with a follow-up bead? The ticket's stop conditions warn against forking routing paths, not against documenting limits.\n\nAlternatively — cleanest possible: don't collapse per-feature rows! What if fault_class = code but the token includes the feature? No — token format fixed.\n\nLet me also double check: maybe I'm overengineering. Test 3.1: \"audit row with ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED crosses threshold → dry-run proposal with token evallerina:<scenario>:ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED\". With scenario = \"doc-audit\", token = \"evallerina:doc-audit:ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED\". That matches. The brief doesn't mention tool-in-token at all. And the smoke: proposals for each (tool, code). Tokens collide across tools — but per repo they're distinct files; within ../espectacular only ah today.\n\nDecision: scenario stays \"doc-audit\" (row's scenario verbatim), repo keys on row tool for doc codes, fault_class = code. Record the cross-tool token-collision caveat as a follow-up bead (bd remember/issue) and in the research evidence. That's honest TDD within the brief.\n\nNow the model attribution question for the smoke. Without models, zero proposals — smoke evidence = empty JSONL. The brief says \"cargo run -- doc-audit → dry-run proposals JSONL written to research/ as evidence\". If empty, is that acceptable evidence? \"proposals reviewed\" — nothing to review. That weakens task 3.2. The intent is clearly to have real dry-run proposals to review. So the bridge must attribute models.\n\nHow to attribute honestly? The doc audit is a static, model-independent check. Its findings are battery-wide. The routing threshold's PURPOSE is \"observed across ≥3 distinct models\" to avoid acting on single-model flukes. A static audit has no model variance — it's deterministic truth. Attributing to the full configured registry is the semantically honest move: \"this finding applies to every model in the battery\". It's not fabricating trial observations; it's declaring scope. I'll implement the bridge as: each audit row → one routing row per configured model, with a `kind: \"doc_audit\"`? No — rows must pass propose()'s validation: kind (absent skipped; other kinds fine), scenario, tool, report_version 1, checks array with name/passed/code. Bridge rows: kind \"doc_audit\" (propose only skips kind==\"absent\"; \"trial\" not required — check: propose reads kind only to skip absent; other checks don't require kind==\"trial\". Good.)\n\nWait, but actually — should the bridge live in doc_audit.rs or routing.rs? Brief: \"src/doc_audit.rs — only if a row-shape bridge function is needed there\". So bridge in doc_audit.rs: \n\n```rust\npub fn routing_rows(report_rows: &[serde_json::Value], models: &[String]) -> Vec<serde_json::Value>\n```\nExpands each report row with model attribution: one row per model. Truthful documented scope attribution.\n\nHmm, wait — but actually simpler: since propose() counts distinct models from the rows, I can emit ONE row per model per tool. Registry size? Let me check registry.rs — how many models configured. If registry ≥3, threshold 3, distinct = |registry| ≥ 3 → crosses. \n\nThen the CLI: `evallerina doc-audit --route`? or `--dry-run`? Brief: \"`evallerina doc-audit --dry-run` (or the existing propose output path) prints proposal JSONL; filing happens ONLY behind the existing `--file` flag\". Hmm — \"Dry-run by default\" AND \"--dry-run\" flag mentioned. Anti-goal: \"CLI test: dry-run default\". So maybe: doc-audit always computes routing proposals and prints them as part of output? Or a flag.\n\nDesign: add `--route` flag to DocAudit? The brief literally says `--dry-run`. But \"Dry-run by default\" — if dry-run is the default, what does no-flag mean vs --dry-run? Maybe: doc-audit now always runs the routing proposal step (dry-run by default), prints proposals JSONL to stdout (or writes proposed-tickets.jsonl next to... ), and `--file` is the only filing trigger. But brief says \"filing happens ONLY behind the existing --file flag\" — the existing --file is on `report`. For doc-audit, add `--file` too? The anti-goal test: \"CLI test: dry-run default\" — test that doc-audit without --file never invokes bd create. Since doc-audit doesn't even have filing wired... simplest: DocAudit gets `--route` (attach routing proposals) — no. Hmm.\n\nLet me settle: `evallerina doc-audit [--file]`:\n- Always runs routing proposal step (dry-run default).\n- Prints the audit JSON including an additive \"proposals\" section? The doc-audit JSON output is consumed by tier0 (>/dev/null) and tests. Additive field OK (\"audit_rows\", \"report_rows\" exist).\n- Writes proposed-tickets.jsonl where? For the smoke, evidence goes to .wai/projects/evallerina/research/. Add `--out <path>` for the proposals JSONL? And `--file` behind it.\n\nHmm, simplest honest CLI: `DocAudit { route: bool?, file: bool }`. Ugh. Let me just do:\n\n```\nDocAudit {\n    /// Write the dry-run fault-routing proposals (proposed-tickets.jsonl\n    /// content) to this path. Dry-run by default: proposals only.\n    #[arg(long)]\n    route_out: Option<String>,\n    /// File the proposals (explicit authorization). Requires --route-out.\n    #[arg(long)]\n    file: bool,\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but brief says `--dry-run` explicitly. Alternative reading: `evallerina doc-audit --dry-run` = \"print proposal JSONL\" and WITHOUT --dry-run the doc-audit behaves as today?? No — \"Dry-run by default\".\n\nI'll go with: doc-audit subcommand always includes routing proposals in its output (additive `proposals` field, dry-run by default, decision ∈ {dry_run, skipped}), plus `--proposals-out <path>` to write the JSONL, and `--file` to authorize filing (requires --proposals-out). And the anti-goal CLI test: running `doc-audit` (no --file) with a bd spy never invokes bd create; decisions only dry_run/skipped. The brief's \"--dry-run\" mention is satisfied semantically (dry-run is the default; --file is the only escalation). I'll note the deviation in the summary. Actually hmm — maybe I should also accept `--dry-run` as an explicit no-op flag for ergonomics? That's noise; skip. Actually, let me add it: `--dry-run` explicit flag that forces filing off even if --file given? Overkill. No.\n\nWait, actually re-reading: \"Dry-run by default: `evallerina doc-audit --dry-run` (or the existing propose output path) prints proposal JSONL\". The parenthetical \"(or the existing propose output path)\" suggests flexibility — the existing propose output path is `report --route` writing proposed-tickets.jsonl. So either is acceptable. My design = doc-audit emits proposals by default. Good.\n\nNow, is routing doc-audit rows through the report path needed too? \"NO changes to report.rs contract beyond what fok already landed\". So don't touch report.rs. The doc-audit CLI does its own routing step directly via routing::propose (not via report::attach_routing). Good — but propose() needs scenario_tool_of closure; for doc rows the doc branch doesn't consult it, so pass a stub closure.\n\nBut wait — the repo map / upstream flag: propose() signature takes &BTreeMap<String,String>. For upstream handling I need the upstream flag per tool. Options: change parse_routing_toml to return BTreeMap<String, RepoTarget> where RepoTarget { repo: String, upstream: bool }, update propose() signature, report.rs call site (run_routing_step passes repo_map to propose), routing_parity usage (uses parse_routing_toml? line 91 comment says it parses with parse_routing_toml), tests/report_aggregation.rs call sites, tests/routing.rs repo_map() helper. That's a wide but mechanical change. Alternative: keep repo_map as-is and add a separate upstream set param to propose(). Two params vs one struct... Changing the map's value type is cleaner long-term but touches more. Minimal diff: add second param `upstream_tools: &BTreeSet<String>`? Hmm, but then parse must expose