The parenthetical \\\"(or the existing propose output path)\\\" suggests flexibility — the existing propose output path is `report --route` writing proposed-tickets.jsonl. So either is acceptable. My design = doc-audit emits proposals by default. Good.\\n\\nNow, is routing doc-audit rows through the report path needed too? \\\"NO changes to report.rs contract beyond what fok already landed\\\". So don't touch report.rs. The doc-audit CLI does its own routing step directly via routing::propose (not via report::attach_routing). Good — but propose() needs scenario_tool_of closure; for doc rows the doc branch doesn't consult it, so pass a stub closure.\\n\\nBut wait — the repo map / upstream flag: propose() signature takes &BTreeMap<String,String>. For upstream handling I need the upstream flag per tool. Options: change parse_routing_toml to return BTreeMap<String, RepoTarget> where RepoTarget { repo: String, upstream: bool }, update propose() signature, report.rs call site (run_routing_step passes repo_map to propose), routing_parity usage (uses parse_routing_toml? line 91 comment says it parses with parse_routing_toml), tests/report_aggregation.rs call sites, tests/routing.rs repo_map() helper. That's a wide but mechanical change. Alternative: keep repo_map as-is and add a separate upstream set param to propose(). Two params vs one struct... Changing the map's value type is cleaner long-term but touches more. Minimal diff: add second param `upstream_tools: &BTreeSet<String>`? Hmm, but then parse must expose both. \\n\\nActually cleaner: parse_routing_toml returns BTreeMap<String, RepoEntry> with fields repo+upstream; propose takes that. Let me check all usages precisely:\\n\\n- src/report.rs:64-69 attach_routing(repo_map: &BTreeMap<String,String>) → passes to propose. And run_routing_step builds it from parse_routing_toml.\\n- src/routing_parity.rs:91 — uses parse_routing_toml? Let me check.\\n- tests/report_aggregation.rs: two sites constructing BTreeMap manually and