(\"doc-audit\"), model, tool = row_tool. Good.\n\nThreshold: doc rows with full-registry attribution → models.len() = |registry| ≥ min(3,|registry|) → crosses. Test 3.1: 3 models → crosses.\n\nDedup test (red step 4): same token twice → one proposal. Via BTreeMap collapse: two rows same (scenario, code, tool) → one proposal with occurrences=2. Test asserts proposals.len()==1. \n\nHmm — wait, \"same fingerprint token twice → one proposal. GREEN.\" Also could test at file level (dedup against open issues) but that's covered by existing tests. The propose-level collapse test suffices per brief (\"a test proves double-proposing the same token yields one proposal\").\n\nNow the bridge in doc_audit.rs:\n\n```rust\n/// Bridge doc-audit report rows into the routing intake (e4x): each\n/// audit row is a static, model-independent finding — it applies to\n/// the whole configured battery — so it is attributed to every\n/// configured model id. This keeps propose()'s cross-model threshold\n/// semantics verbatim (no new routing semantics, design D3): a\n/// battery-wide audit finding is battery-wide by construction.\npub fn routing_rows(report_rows: &[serde_json::Value], models: &[String]) -> Vec<serde_json::Value> {\n    report_rows.iter().flat_map(|row| {\n        models.iter().map(|m| {\n            let mut r = row.clone();\n            r[\"kind\"] = json!(\"doc_audit\");\n            r[\"model\"] = json!(m);\n            r\n        })\n    }).collect()\n}\n```\n\nWait — but with the bridge, tool wai rows: report_row(tool, rows) has tool field = wai. propose doc branch keys repo on row_tool = wai. routing.toml has [wai] repo ../wai. Good. bd/openspec: surface none → no tokens → no rows → nothing routed. But upstream test: construct a doc row for tool \"bd\" directly in the routing test with repo_map containing bd upstream=true → expect skipped/unmapped_upstream.\n\nHmm wait — what about tools in doc-audit whose repo is unmapped or upstream? All doc_audit.toml tools ⊆ routing.toml tools (bd, openspec have surface none). But upstream flag must be respected: if hypothetically an upstream tool had audit rows → skipped/unmapped_upstream. Red step 3 tests this at the propose() level. Good.\n\nNow decision logic in propose():\n\n```rust\nlet repo_entry = repo_map.get(&key.tool);\nlet (decision, reason) = match repo_entry {\n    Some(t) if t.upstream => (\"skipped\", Some(\"unmapped_upstream\")),\n    Some(t) => (\"dry_run\", None) with repo Some(t.repo),\n    None => (\"skipped\", Some(\"unmapped_tool\")),\n};\n```\n\nCareful — existing behavior for mapped tool: repo = Some(repo), decision dry_run. Keep. Upstream: repo — should it be Some(repo) or None? Spec: \"decision is skipped with reason unmapped_upstream; no filing\". repo field: I'd set Some(repo path) for information? Or None? The schema says repo: \"Mapped repository path (null until/unless the tool is mapped)\". Upstream tools ARE mapped (parity requires the entry). I'll set repo = Some(repo) and decision skipped/unmapped_upstream — the map knows the repo; filing is refused by policy. Hmm, but file_proposals only files decision==\"dry_run\", so repo value is harmless. I'll include it — more reviewable. Wait but test 3 asserts decision skipped/reason unmapped_upstream; repo unspecified. Fine.\n\nNow file_proposals: no changes needed (skipped rows untouched). But — anti-goal \"upstream=true tools are NEVER filed — pinned by test\": test runs file_proposals(authorized) on an upstream row and asserts zero create calls + decision stays skipped.\n\nCLI wiring in main.rs run_doc_audit: after computing report_rows, run routing:\n\n```rust\n// Load routing.toml, parse repo map, configured models from registry.\nlet routing_toml = fs::read_to_string(root_path.join(\"routing.toml\"))?;\nlet repo_map = routing::parse_routing_toml(&routing_toml)?;\nlet models: Vec<String> = ordered_ids()...;\nlet intake = doc_audit::routing_rows(&report_rows, &models);\nlet mut proposals = routing::propose(&intake, &models, &|_| None, &repo_map)?;\nrouting::file_proposals(&mut proposals, file, &mut runner)?;\n```\n\nWait — scenario_tool_of for doc rows never consulted (doc branch), but rows... all checks in doc-audit rows carry codes (report_row sets code on every check). So the None branch never fires → scenario_tool_of never called. Pass `|_| None`. But hmm — if somehow a code-less check existed, loud error \"scenario 'doc-audit' has no recorded tool\" — correct loudness.\n\nFiling in doc-audit: should `--file` even exist on doc-audit? Brief: \"filing happens ONLY behind the existing --file flag\". For doc-audit I'll add --file flag that goes through file_proposals. Anti-goal test: default (no --file) → file_proposals(false) → zero bd calls. \n\nOutput: add to doc-audit JSON: \"proposals\": [rows.to_json()] (additive). And write JSONL to --proposals-out path if given? For smoke evidence, I can redirect stdout... the audit JSON is pretty-printed single JSON; proposals inside it. The evidence file should be the proposals JSONL. Add `--proposals-out <path>` writing routing::to_jsonl(&proposals). For smoke: `cargo run -- doc-audit --proposals-out .wai/projects/evallerina/research/proposed-doc-tickets-e4x.jsonl`. Hmm, name: proposed-tickets.jsonl convention. Use `proposed-tickets-doc-audit-e4x.jsonl`? The brief: \"proposals JSONL written to .wai/projects/evallerina/research/ as evidence\". I'll name `proposed-tickets.jsonl` inside a dated dir? Just `proposed-tickets-doc-audit.jsonl`. Fine.\n\nAlso `--route` flag? No: dry-run by default, always route. tier0 runs `doc-audit > /dev/null` — routing must not fail there. propose errors if configured_models empty (registry non-empty, fine). Unknown scenario errors — doc rows don't consult. Version 1 fine. OK.\n\nBut wait — one more: tier0 doc-audit now parses routing.toml. In CI standalone clones routing.toml is checked in. Fine.\n\nEdge: doc-audit runs in repos where sibling checkouts missing → those tools produce no rows (repo_absent) → nothing routed. Fine.\n\nNow — tests/routing.rs new tests (RED first). Also a CLI test for dry-run default (anti-goal): where do CLI tests live? tests/ablate_cli.rs exists for ablate. There's tests/routing.rs. A CLI test spawning `cargo run`? ablate_cli.rs — let me check how it tests CLI. Probably uses std::process::Command with the built binary (env!(\"CARGO_BIN_EXE_evallerina\")). I'll add a CLI test asserting `doc-audit` output decisions ∈ {dry_run, skipped} and no bd create — via the binary (no filing possible without --file since doc-audit --file would file in real repos... dangerous test; better: assert the binary without --file never runs bd create — can't spy easily on subprocess. Alternative: test at library level: file_proposals(false) does nothing (already pinned by existing tests? maybe). For CLI: assert output JSON proposals decisions are dry_run/skipped only. And assert that `doc-audit` (no --file) exit 0. That pins dry-run default shape.\n\nLet me check ablate_cli.rs pattern quickly, lib.rs, registry.rs, and routing_parity's use of parse_routing_toml.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Real audit rows exist: 91 rows across 9 tools. Good — the smoke will have real findings.\\n\\nNow the model-attribution question. The doc-audit report rows have no \\\"model\\\" field. For propose() to cross the threshold with real doc-audit rows, the rows need model attribution. Hmm.\\n\\nWait, let me reconsider. Maybe the doc-audit routing intake should attribute rows... Let me re-read the brief's desired outcome 1 again: \\\"a row with code ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED or ERR_DOC_NOT_FINDABLE routes like any other ERR_* agent-fault row — fingerprint token evallerina:<scenario>:<fault-class>, cross-model threshold min(3, |registry|) (existing propose() semantics reused verbatim — no new routing semantics per D3).\\\"\\n\\nAnd task 3.2: \\\"Dry-run smoke against latest tier-2 rows; review proposals before any filing\\\" — wait the tasks.md says \\\"latest tier-2 rows\\\"! But the ticket prompt says \\\"task 3.2 smoke: cargo run -- doc-audit → dry-run proposals JSONL\\\". tasks.md 3.2: \\\"Dry-run smoke against latest tier-2 rows; review proposals before any filing (mirrors 4.3 of add-fault-ticket-routing)\\\".\\n\\nThe brief overrides: \\\"Dry-run smoke against the latest real doc-audit rows (`cargo run -- doc-audit` output)\\\". So the smoke uses doc-audit rows. For proposals to appear, doc-audit rows must cross the threshold.\\n\\nSo how do doc-audit rows cross a distinct-model threshold with no models? Option A: they don't — smoke produces zero proposals (empty JSONL). Option B: the bridge attributes each audit row to the configured registry (all configured models) — since the audit is a static, model-independent check, its finding applies to the entire battery. Is that a \\\"new routing semantic\\\"? It's an intake-shape decision: the bridge function in doc_audit.rs expands rows with model attribution = full registry. That is honest-ish: the audit finding is attributed to the whole configured battery, not fabricated per-model observations. But provenance rows would list models that never ran... eh.\\n\\nHmm. Option C: propose() treats doc-code rows as exempt from the model threshold — i.e., a single occurrence routes. That IS new semantics (threshold bypass) — violates D3.\\n\\nOption D: doc-audit rows carry \\\"model\\\" = each configured model via bridge in doc_audit.rs. This reuses propose() verbatim — the threshold counts distinct models = |registry| ≥ min(3, |registry|) → crosses. The provenance is truthful in the sense \\\"this finding is battery-wide\\\". But the rows claim models observed trials that didn't run... but provenance rows just record (scenario, model, tool) pairs from the bridged rows. It's a deliberate bridge, documented.\\n\\nThe brief says: \\\"src/doc_audit.rs audit rows are accepted as fault rows by the routing layer\\\" and \\\"Exact files: src/doc_audit.rs — only if a