lling attach_routing presumably.\\n- tests/routing.rs: repo_map() helper + propose calls.\\n\\nThat's manageable. But is changing the public propose() signature \\\"minimal diff\\\"? The brief says upstream=true tools emit skipped/unmapped_upstream proposal rows — pinned by test. This is required behavior (red step 3). So signature change is warranted.\\n\\nHmm wait — actually, maybe simpler: keep repo_map: BTreeMap<String,String>, and handle upstream via a new param on propose: `upstream_tools: &BTreeSet<String>` defaulting empty in callers? Every existing caller passes &BTreeSet::new(). Also mechanical. But two parallel maps risk desync; the struct map is better. I'll do the struct: \\n\\n```rust\\npub struct RepoTarget { pub repo: String, pub upstream: bool }\\npub fn parse_routing_toml(content: &str) -> Result<BTreeMap<String, RepoTarget>, String>\\n```\\n\\nCheck routing_parity.rs usage first.\\n\\nAlso note: file_proposals — should upstream rows ever file? propose() marks them skipped/unmapped_upstream; file_proposals only touches decision==\\\"dry_run\\\" rows. So upstream never files. Good — anti-goal pinned by test: upstream row stays skipped after file_proposals with file_authorized=true and zero bd create calls.\\n\\nNow the propose() changes in detail:\\n\\n```rust\\nconst DOC_CODES: ... — or use doc_audit constants. routing.rs referencing doc_audit::ERR_* — fine (same crate, lib module cycle? doc_audit and routing are sibling modules; routing can use crate::doc_audit::ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED).\\n```\\n\\nWait — should routing.rs depend on doc_audit.rs, or define its own set? The codes are doc_audit's contract constants. Use crate::doc_audit::{ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED, ERR_DOC_NOT_FINDABLE}. lib.rs exposes both? Check lib.rs.\\n\\nIn the checks loop:\\n\\n```rust\\nmatch check.get(\\\"code\\\").and_then(serde_json::Value::as_str) {\\n    // Doc-coverage faults (evallerina-fok): routable tool-repo faults.\\n    // The audited tool is the row's `tool` fie