anti-goal: no filing without --file). New test in tests/routing.rs or a doc-audit CLI test file. I'll add to tests/routing.rs a test at library level? The anti-goal says \"pinned by test (CLI test: dry-run default)\". CLI test: run the binary `doc-audit` and assert output proposals decisions ∈ {dry_run, skipped}. Doc-audit real run requires the sibling repos — available in this workspace. But in CI standalone? The binary test depends on sibling checkouts... tier0 already runs doc-audit. tests run in CI with siblings per D2 GHA. Hmm, tests/ablate_cli style: spawn binary. doc-audit run takes ~seconds. OK.\n\nActually a cleaner CLI anti-goal test: run `evallerina doc-audit` in a sandbox temp dir (not the repo root)? doc-audit discovers root via git rev-parse --show-toplevel from cwd — sandbox isn't a git repo → error. Could `git init` a sandbox with doc_audit.toml+routing.toml, and a fake tool binary... that's a hermetic CLI test of the routing path: create sandbox git repo with doc_audit.toml pointing to a fixture binary and repo slice, routing.toml mapping; run binary `doc-audit`; assert proposals decisions dry_run, and no bd anywhere (no .beads). That's heavier but hermetic. Hmm. Given effort, maybe: hermetic sandbox test with a tiny fixture. Let me gauge: doc_audit.toml [fixtool] repo=\".\" binary=\"fixtool\" where fixtool is a shell script printing --help with a flag; doc surfaces: README.md missing the flag → ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED row; routing.toml [fixtool] repo=\"../nowhere\"? unmapped → skipped/unmapped_tool. For dry_run decision need mapped repo — repo = \".\" (mapped). Then proposals decision dry_run. Assert no filing: no bd calls — the sandbox has no beads; file_proposals(false) does nothing anyway. The test asserts decisions and that no .beads dir was created. Good enough as \"CLI dry-run default\" pin. Also assert with --file? No — --file in test would invoke real bd in sandbox... bd not initialized → bd_failure skipped. Could assert that too: `--file` in a non-beads mapped repo → skipped/bd_failure, never crash. Nice.\n\nHmm wait — the sandbox needs `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` to work → git init the sandbox. discover_root runs git in cwd — Command inherits cwd of the process; the test can .current_dir(sandbox). Output JSON parsed. Doable.\n\nBut also — fixture binary must be runnable: create an executable script in sandbox, add to PATH via env. doc_audit extract_surface runs Command::new(\"fixtool\") — resolves via PATH. Set .env(\"PATH\", sandbox). Binary script with shebang. Repo \".\" contains README.md without the token → audit row. doc_audit.toml + routing.toml in sandbox root.\n\nThis is a solid hermetic CLI test. Write it as red (Command::Smoke... no, `doc-audit` exists already but without proposals field → red on missing \"proposals\" key / missing JSONL). \n\nNow implementation order and commits:\n\n1. Commit A (RED→GREEN step 1): test threshold→proposal for ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED; implement doc branch (first code only? or both). I'll implement branch with just ERR_FEATURE_UNDOCUMENTED, green, commit. Hmm — but the branch code with one code then extending is a bit silly. Alternatively implement both and write both tests before implementing... Brief explicitly lists them as steps 1 and 2. I'll keep the two-commit shape but implement both codes in commit 1? No — then test 2 is green at commit 1 and step 2's red never happens. Strict: commit 1 handles code 1 only. I'll do that — it's 1 extra edit.\n\nHmm, wait — actually, let me reconsider combining: real-world pragmatism vs brief fidelity. The brief is explicit: \"Red/green sequence: 1. RED ... GREEN. 2. RED ... GREEN.\" I'll follow it literally. Fine.\n\n2. Commit B (step 2): add ERR_DOC_NOT_FINDABLE + test. \n\n3. Commit C (step 3): upstream test (requires RoutingEntry type change in propose signature + parse switch + report.rs/test updates) — red via compile/test failure, then implement. Actually the type change IS the implementation; test written first won't compile against old API → red (compile error). Then implement: change signature, reuse routing_parity::RoutingEntry, update report.rs + tests. Also doc rows for upstream: decision skipped/unmapped_upstream. Green, commit.\n\n4. Commit D (step 4): dedup test (expect already green — characterization pin), commit.\n\n5. Commit E (step 5): bridge function in doc_audit.rs + CLI wiring + hermetic CLI tests (dry-run default; --file never files upstream; maybe bd_failure). Then smoke run, evidence file, commit. Also openspec tasks 3.1/3.2 checkoff (commit with evidence or separate). Also research note.\n\nAlso must keep report.rs untouched except the repo_map type (that's routing machinery, allowed — \"NO changes to report.rs contract beyond what fok already landed\" — the type change is not a contract change; dashboard shape unchanged. attach_routing signature is internal API. OK.)\n\nToken collision caveat: I'll record a bd follow-up issue at the end? The beads rules require export+commit after any bd change. Simpler: record in the research note (org note? notes in .org format per AGENTS — research note in .wai/projects/evallerina/research/). The brief says evidence committed as research. I'll write the proposals JSONL + a short .org review note. Also file a bd issue for the collision follow-up... The prompt says commit everything; filing bd issues — allowed? bd is fine (issue tracking, not cross-repo filing). But careful: bd create in evallerina repo. The completion requires bd export invariant commit. I'll do one follow-up issue for the token-collision-over-shared-repo caveat + tasks 4.x remaining? No — don't file unrelated. One focused follow-up: \"doc-audit routing tokens collide across tools sharing a repo\". Do it at the end with export commit.\n\nWait, also — should the smoke include review of proposals? \"proposals reviewed and committed as evidence\". I'll review in the note (e.g., spot-check wai's 2 rows against routing.toml map, decision counts, upstream tools absent).\n\nLet me also double-check propose()'s handling of bridged rows: kind \"doc_audit\" — kind != \"absent\" → proceeds. report_version 1 ✓. scenario \"doc-audit\" ✓. tool ✓. model ✓. checks have name/passed/code ✓.\n\nOne more check — `report_row` sets \"status\": \"failed\" — propose doesn't read status. OK.\n\nAlso main.rs currently: `Command::DocAudit` no fields → `Command::DocAudit => ...`. Will become `Command::DocAudit { proposals_out, file }`.\n\nWait, also decide flag names. Brief: `--dry-run` \"(or the existing propose output path)\". I'm choosing: proposals are computed always (dry-run default); `--proposals-out <path>` writes JSONL; `--file` authorizes filing (requires --proposals-out? no — file can work without writing the jsonl; but writing the record is good. I'll require --proposals-out for --file? The report path writes proposed-tickets.jsonl automatically. For symmetry: doc-audit --file writes proposed-tickets.jsonl next to --proposals-out? Overcomplicating. Keep: --proposals-out writes the JSONL (post-filing decisions, so the artifact records what happened); --file authorizes filing; without --proposals-out, proposals appear only in the stdout JSON (\"proposals\" array). --file without --proposals-out allowed. Fine.\n\nAlso update the doc-audit doc-comment in main.rs and possibly the JSON top-level: add \"proposals\": [...], \"routing_threshold\"? Add threshold? Keep minimal: \"proposals\" array of row.to_json(). Hmm — the stdout JSON is checked by tier0 (>/dev/null) and maybe tests/doc_audit.rs? Let me check tests/doc_audit.rs for output-shape pins. It tests library functions probably. And tests/ablate_cli? Not doc-audit. Let me grep quickly for \"proposals\" or doc-audit CLI tests.\n\nAlso: routing.rs top doc-comment should be updated to mention doc-coverage intake (e4x). And doc_audit.rs Purpose header mention bridge.\n\nThreshold note: routing threshold min(3, |registry|) with registry of 4 → 3. Bridged rows: 4 models each → 4 distinct ≥ 3 → crosses. \n\nNow — one subtlety in propose(): the doc branch uses row_tool for repo keying, and tool-fault branch uses recorded_tool (lazy). For the bridged CLI path, rows have scenario \"doc-audit\"; no code-less checks; scenario_tool_of never called; pass |_: &str| None.\n\nLet me now also confirm there's no existing test pinning \"unknown scenario errors even when all checks pass or only doc rows\". Grep tests for \"has no recorded tool\". Also check tests/report_aggregation.rs around 440-470 & 610-660 to update types."}}
